ng Dining Services Residence Life Lyon Education & Adventure Program Wright Wilderness Experience Outdoor Trips & Events Bouldering Wall Challenge Course Disc Golf Course Hiking and Biking Trail Gallery Rental Gear Waivers & Evals LEAP DEI and EEC Information Lyon League Lyon Lift Mobile App Need Help With An Event? Pet Life Pet Life FAQ Pet Life Press Release Pet Policy Campus Pet Requirements Letter From Presidential Pet Campus Pet Application Form Stay Connected Alumni, Parents, & Friends 1872 Society Alumni Association Alumni Awards Alumni Personnel Donor Honor Roll How to Give Stay Involved Why Your Gift Matters Advancement Honor Roll of Donors Alumni Giving The Honor Roll of Donors Mobile App Student Handbook Student Involvement Campus Employment Religious Life Civic Engagement Lyon College Recyclers Greek Life Alpha Xi Delta Chi Omicron Kappa Sigma Phi Mu Tau Kappa Epsilon SAC Student Government Student Organizations Black Student Association MLK Day MLK Day Program Student Life Dean of Students Fitness Facilities Game Room Cardio Room Free Store SOS Pantry Scots Spirit Wear Store The Social and Honor System Title IX Title IX Personnel Title IX Policy Title IX FAQs File a Title IX Report Title IX Support and Resources The Wheelbarrow Contact Resources Contact Us Event Request Application Give Job Opportunities Marketing Request News Report a Concern Information Technology Information Technology IT by the Numbers IT Announcements FAQs/Knowledgebase Governance Links Footer Right Nav Course & Exam Schedules Stay Connected Style Guide Net Price Calculator The Highlander Newspaper Community Calendar News Request a Transcript Schedule a Tour Mobile menu and search toggle Search We can't wait to meet you Schedule a visit Apply to Lyon Honors Fellows Program Capstone Podcast Projects Lyon College's Honors 400 course is a capstone experience designed for Honors Fellows to practice communicating outside of the bubbles of technical jargon in order to reach a wider public. The course is intended to celebrate the interdisciplinarity, public engagement, and purposeful education that are central to the liberal arts. For the final assignment of the course, each student develops a 10-20 minute podcast aimed at communicating a complex idea from their field of study to a general audience. Morgan Le Fay: Chivalry's Hidden Threat with Annie Blevins View More \"Checks and Balances\" with Madeline Cleaves View More Depression Showdown: Discussing the Battle Over Depression’s Origins with Hannah Davison View More Sweet Science, Bitter Results with Zane Fountain View More Kolmogorov–Arnold Networks for Tabular Data  Synthesis with Cristian Del Gobbo View More Intertwined with Misogyny: The Origin of the Weaponization of Biblical Texts with Madeline Hopson View More Cover to Cover with Natalya Kaza View More The Economical Impact on Domestic Violence with Katelyn Mace View More The Grey Area with Zoe Papazoglou View More Perception and Economic Voting Theory with Beau Turner View More Morgan Le Fay: Chivalry's Hidden Threat Podcast Abstract: On this podcast episode, Morgan Le Fay: Chivalry’s Hidden Threat, we will discuss the history of the mythological character Morgan Le Fay and analyze her position in the medieval poem Sir Gawain and the Green Knight. In this poem written by the author known as the Pearl Poet, Morgan creates a game to trick one of King Arthur’s knights, Gawain, and lead him astray from the ideals of chivalry. Morgan’s control over Gawain and the narrative of the story makes her the most powerful character in the poem; however, she is not revealed as the puppet master or even a character until the end, despite her being responsible for the entirety of the plot of the poem. Many readers have noted how this delayed revelation strategically overshadows Morgan's power and control over the narrative, attempting to obscure the fact that a female figure is responsible for defaming knighthood as an institution. In this episode, we will discuss why this obscurity is necessary to hide the reality of the weaknesses of knighthood and patriarchal dominance, both relying on women to be able to perform their duties. Morgan playing the role of the puppet master throughout this poem symbolizes the real threat to King Arthur’s court, knighthood, chivalry, and male power: women. \"Morgan Le Fay: Chivalry's Hidden Threat\" with Annie Blevins Student Bio: My name is Annie Blevins, and I am from Violet Hill, Arkansas. I am majoring in English and Spanish, and I have served as an executive member of the Alpha Xi Delta sorority and The Wheelbarrow literary arts magazine during my time at Lyon. After graduation, I plan to attend graduate programs and pursue an MA and PhD in English Rhetoric and Composition. \"Checks and Balances\" Podcast Abstract: On this episode of \"Checks and Balances,” we explore a question that has severe implications on the last 10 years of U.S. politics: How does social media proliferate extremist values?. Alongside the ever-expanding and seemingly impossible-to-avoid influence of social media, there has been an observable uptick in violent extremism. As of 2025, 253 million social media users reside in the United States, which accounts for 73 percent of the total U.S. population. Concurrently, the evolving accessibility of social media coincides with a rise in political polarization, marking an extreme uptick in radicalization, violent extremism, and the erosion of democratic norms. In today's episode, we explore the notion that social media has a direct correlation with real-world political violence and influences the rate at which these acts of violence occur through the exploitation of social media as a weapon for spreading extremist values. We review some of the most significant examples of violent extremism in the United States over the last 5 years and how social media may have played a part in their manifestation. Additionally, we explore how a certain figurehead in right-wing extremist circles is on their way to becoming a household name– and how he achieved this through the exploitation of social media. \"Checks and Balances\" with Madeline Cleaves Student Bio: My name is Madeline Cleaves, and I am from Maumelle, Arkansas. I will be graduating from Lyon in Political Science in Spring 2026! During my time in the political science department, I have done research on the relationship between social media and violent extremism under Dr. Sung, and am currently conducting research on if the increasing implementation of harm reduction legislation has had a positive impact on overdose mortality rates in the United States. In addition to my research, I have stayed involved on campus through the Alpha Xi Delta Sorority: serving as Philanthropy Vice President, Panhellenic Delegate, and Chaplin during my four years of membership. Post-Lyon, I plan to attend Law School! Depression Showdown: Discussing the Battle Over Depression’s Origins Podcast Abstract: Who will win? Clinical depression, also known as major depression or major depressive disorder (MDD), is defined as extensive feelings of sadness, emptiness or hopelessness with physical, social and emotional manifestations. Manifestations include irritable mood, loss of interest in activities, anxiety, weight loss or gain, sleep disturbances, tiredness, and suicidal thoughts. In this battle royale podcast, we are going to pin two evolutionary theories of depression against each other and determine which one reigns supreme! In one corner, representing MDD as an adaptive benefit, is the Social Competition Hypothesis. This theory was proposed by Price and colleagues that the depressive state we witness today evolved as a strategy for an individual to accept defeat. In the other corner, also representing an adaptive feature for depression, is the Defense Against Infectious Disease Theory. This theory was proposed by Kinney and Tanaka, and they argue that depression prevents the spread of a disease and reduces energy expenditure. I will also toss my own theory into the ring that MDD is a maladaptive trait rather than adaptive. Understanding the evolutionary origins of depression would provide insight into improving treatment methods for the disorder and further the understanding of mental illness. Join me as three theories enter: only one survives! \"Depression Showdown: Discussing the Battle Over Depression’s Origins\" with Hannah Davison Student Bio: I am a senior biology major with a pre-medical concentration and a psychology minor. I am a member of the Lyon College Concert and Marching band. I am involved in the Honor Council, Mortar Board, Chemistry Club, Honors Fellows, French Club, Alpha Chi, Psi Chi, Bible College Ministries and research on campus. After graduation, I plan to attend medical school. Sweet Science, Bitter Results Podcast Synopsis: In recent years artificial sweeteners have become a large part of the human diet. One of which is sucralose, or Splenda, a non-calorie synthetic sweetener, that is about 600 times as sweet as regular sugar. It is often marketed as a “lite or diet” option because it cannot be broken down by the human body. Thus these combined properties as well as its increased stability across a wide pH range make it incredibly useful in the food, beverage, and pharmaceutical industries. Sucralose has been determined safe for consumption in more than sixty countries, but studies in recent years have determined it to inhibit bacteria in the environment as well as in the human gut. Using the knowledge of these inhibitory effects, we attempted to develop new derivatives of the sucralose molecule and determine their antibacterial properties. Due to the unpredictable nature of “sugar chemistry,” we were unable to synthesize and purify any of the derivatives that we hoped to create. Although we were ultimately unsuccessful in synthesizing a bacteria-inhibitory drug from sucralose, we were still able to advance the science of drug synthesis by showing people what has been tried and what results came from that. \"Sweet Science, Bitter Results\" with Zane Fountain Student Bio: My name is Zane Fountain and I am a biology and chemistry double major from Calico Rock, Arkansas. After completing my degree at Lyon, I plan to go to optometry school to pursue a career as an optometrist. While at Lyon, I have been a part of the Honors Fellows Program, TKE, SGA, Honor Council, Mortar Board, and Chemistry Club. I have also been a research student in Dr. Irosha’s research lab since Fall 2023. I hope you enjoy my podcast! Kolmogorov–Arnold Networks for Tabular Data  Synthesis Podcast Synopsis: Synthetic data is becoming increasingly important in today’s AI-driven world, especially in sensitive domains where using real data raises privacy or ethical concerns. In this podcast, I present my research, which investigates a new way to generate high-quality synthetic tabular data. Traditional synthetic data genera- tor models use standard neural networks called Multilayer Perceptrons (MLPs). My project explores what happens when we replace those with a new type of neural network called Kolmogorov-Arnold Networks (KANs), which aims to capture more complex patterns in the data. To evaluate this idea, I created six KAN-based models and tested them against their traditional versions. I looked at three key aspects: how well they reproduce the structure of real data (statistical fidelity), how useful the synthetic data is for training machine learning models (ML utility), and how efficient the models are in terms of memory and speed. The results show that KAN-based models can outperform traditional ones in quality and utility, but they’re more computationally expensive. The models are available as an open-source Python package called KAN-synth. \"Kolmogorov-Arnold Networks for Tabular Data Synthesis\" with Cristian Del Gobbo Student Bio: My name is Cristian Del Gobbo, and I am from Pedaso, a little town on the Adriatic Sea in Italy. I am majoring in Mathematics, Computer Science, and Data Science. During my time at Lyon, I have been involved in several academic and leadership organizations, including serving as the President and Founder of the Bitcoin Club, President of the International Student Association, and Vice President of the Consulting Club. I have also been inducted into honor societies such as Alpha Lambda Delta, Mortar Board, and Alpha Chi, and have worked as a Supplemental Instructor and math tutor. After graduation, I plan to pursue a Master’s degree in Applied Mathematics or a related field. Intertwined with Misogyny: The Origin of the Weaponization of Biblical Texts Podcast Synopsis: \"Intertwined with Misogyny: The Origin of the Weaponization of Biblical Texts\" explores how the English translation of the Bible commissioned by King James I continues to shape religious and cultural attitudes toward women and marginalized groups. The first segment of the podcast features a conversation with Dr. Melissa Johnson, who provides background information on King James’s rise to power, his role in the witch trials, and his political motivations behind producing an English Bible translation. Together, we examine how the King James Version (KJV) intertwined political ambition, gendered power, and theological authority to justify misogyny under the guise of divine order. We break down how the translation process reflected King James’s desire to solidify his authority, align himself with the Protestant movement, and embed his own patriarchal and anti-women perspectives into a religious text. In the second half of the podcast, my second guest, Bryanna Rosario, and I discuss how remnants of this misogynistic framing persist in modern religious discourse, particularly within Christian Nationalist movements that use scripture to rationalize discrimination. By tracing the historical roots of biblical weaponization to King James’s personal and political agenda, this podcast invites listeners to critically re-examine how sacred texts can be manipulated to perpetuate inequality and exclusion. Additionally, the podcast urges listeners to reconsider the ethical and cultural implications of accepting sacred texts without interrogating their origins. The goal of this podcast is not to discredit faith, but to foster a more informed and critical understanding of how religious texts have been interpreted and weaponized over time. \"Intertwined with Misogyny: The Origin of the Weaponization of Biblical Texts\" with Madeline Hopson Student Bio: My name is Madeline Hopson, and I am graduating in December 2025. I was born in Henderson, Texas but moved to Flippin, Arkansas during middle school. I am double majoring in Political Science and Psychology with a Pre-Law concentration. My involvement at  Lyon includes being a four-year member of the Lyon Volleyball team, numerous honor societies, Executive Editor for the Wheelbarrow, Writing Center Tutor & Supplemental Instructor/Observer for the MAC, Year One Mentor, Lyon College Ambassador, and lastly, co-founder and president of the Lyon College Young Progressives. Additionally, I am a member of several clubs on campus, such as the Political Science Club, Law Students' Association, Sand Volleyball Club, and the Gardening Club. I have presented three research projects at numerous conferences. After Lyon, I plan on attending law school in August of 2026. Cover to Cover Podcast Synopsis: Literature lies at the heart of a society's culture, providing an intimacy into the everyday lives of ordinary individuals. Although sometimes limited, translation serves as a mechanism to bridge the gap between worldviews, crossing the boundaries created by the complexity of language. Literary translation has long impacted the way I see the world as since childhood I have benefited from reading pieces from around the globe. My first hand experience with physical translation began the spring semester of my sophomore year when I began aiding in the initial translation of the 2023 Nicaraguan novel, Desventura, by Karly Gaitán Morales from Spanish to English and the subsequent editing process. On a basic level, the story revolves around the murder of a young woman and the ensuing search to locate her killer, but within its pages an important historical context is revealed. Beginning in the late 1970s, the growing conflict between the Somozas and Sandinistas in Nicaragua erupted into a full scale political revolution. The plot follows the recurring historical implications of this event on everyday lives. This experience has helped me to better understand not only the impact, but the difficulty of crossing a language barrier. Every story provides a unique viewpoint, and translation ensures that this primary message can be enjoyed by a wider readership. \"Cover to Cover\" with Natalya Kaza Student Bio: My name is Natalya Kaza. I grew up in Little Rock, AR and I am a double major in Biology and Spanish with a pre-veterinary concentration. My campus involvement includes serving as the president of the Honors Fellows Program, Vice President of the Honor Council, Vice President of Mortar Board, and as a member of SAAC, SGA and the Women’s Basketball Team. After graduation, I plan to attend veterinary school. The Economical Impact on Domestic Violence Podcast Synopsis: In a world of unpredictability, econometrics is a tool that many economists value and utilize in their everyday research. Econometrics is the union of mathematics, economics, and statistics that allow the cross analyzation of data to predict theoretical political and economic trends and policies. This application can be utilized by economists, researchers, politicians, and everyday people alike to predict future movements in an ever changing world. In today’s episode, we will dive into the intersection between poverty levels and domestic violence rates throughout the United States by cross-analyzing a state’s economic environment (education levels and unemployment rates) with their reported domestic violence cases. Using data sources that cross analyze a state’s domestic violence rates with the economic factors that contribute to a state’s wealth and prosperity level, we can see if there is any true correlation between the two. This podcast will show the ways in which econometrics can be used to predict relationships between two aspects, and show the ways in which policies can be formed by these predictions. \"The Economical Impact on Domestic Violence\" with Katelyn Mace Student Bio: My name is Katelyn Mace, and I am from Little Rock, Arkansas. I will be graduating from Lyon with a Business degree and Economics minor. As a student, I have served as an executive member of the Alpha Xi Delta Zeta Iota chapter and run as treasurer for the International Travel Club, promoting the Lyon Poland Trip that I myself have attended. After I graduate, I intend to go to Law School. The Grey Area Podcast Synopsis: Efforts to grant rights to the environment are inherently complex, intertwining ethical considerations with legal frameworks. While the United States legal system has traditionally prioritized human needs to the detriment of other species, many societies conceptualize nature as a network of social beings in reciprocal relationships with humans. This perspective, combined with increasing environmental destruction, has motivated activists and legal scholars to advocate for Rights of Nature (RoN)—the extension of legal rights to biotic entities. This podcast examines the research question: Can the important ethical reasoning behind the Rights of Nature (RoN) approach be translated into a workable legal paradigm that is effective as an environmental protection strategy? Rather than providing a purely theoretical critique, the podcast explores how RoN functions in practice across national contexts, including Ecuador and New Zealand, considering where it succeeds, where it falls short, and why. Using an analytical method grounded in political and legal theory, I assess three areas that present challenges for translating RoN into statutes: scope and precision, the creation of boundaries, and question of adjudication. Additionally, I will further analyze RoN from diverse perspectives, featuring guests to facilitate this discussion: one with a background in political science and mathematics, and another with an exercise science major with an interest in wildlife and nature. Together, we examine how different cultural worldviews shape understandings of the human-nature relationship and influence the plausibility of RoN strategies. This discussion invites listeners to reconsider what we mean by rights, responsibility, and the natural world itself. \"The Grey Area\" with Zoe Papazoglou Student Bio: My name is Zoe Papazoglou and I am from Long Island, New York. I am double majoring in Political Science and Psychology with a pre-law concentration. At Lyon, I have been the captain of the women's soccer team, president of the Student Government Association, a Residential Assistant, a Year One mentor, as well as a Supplemental Instructor and Peer Academic Coach for the MAC. Additionally I have held membership in multiple organizations on campus including Alpha Lambda Delta, Alpha Chi, Mortar Board, Social Council, and the Honors Fellow program. After graduation, I intend on going to law school. Perception and Economic Voting Theory Podcast Synopsis: This podcast will delve into the possibility that the actual state of the economy has significantly less impact on presidential elections than voters’ perceptions of the economy. Using the University of Michigan Consumer Sentiment Index, it will explore how emotion, bias, and the other aspects of bounded rationality impact real world political outcomes as much or more than figures and facts. While traditional models used in the harder social sciences, such as political science and economics, still primarily rely on rational choice theory—which, in this case, leads to economic voting models relying on GDP measures and unemployment rates, this research supports that voters more so rely on subjective evaluations, which leads to the perceived economic performance diverging from the hard economic data. This podcast draws on decades of data and theory in behavioral economics to explain this. It is a deep dive into the impact of bounded rationality on American democracy, showing that while elections are largely still determined by the economy, how individuals perceive the said economy is just as or more important. This podcast will connect the dots between numbers and narratives and attempt to better the understanding of political decision-making. \"Perception and Economic Voting\" with Beau Turner Student Bio: My name is Beau Turner, and I’m from Mountain View, Arkansas. I’m a member of the Lyon Scots baseball team, and I’m currently pursuing a double major in political science and economics, along with a minor in French. I’m a member of the Lyon College Honors Fellowship program and am involved in several campus organizations, including the French Club, Chess Club, and Alpha Chi and Pi Sigma Alpha honor societies. After graduating from Lyon, I plan to attend graduate school to study public policy, with the long-term goal of contributing to meaningful change in society through research, analysis, and leadership. Lyon College Start Your Future Lyon is more than just a college. It's a community distinguished by its academic curriculum, unique honor and social systems, and award-winning professors. Apply Today Contact Resources Contact Us Event Request Application Give Job Opportunities Marketing Request News Report a Concern Footer Right Nav Course & Exam Schedules Stay Connected Style Guide Net Price Calculator The Highlander Newspaper Community Calendar tik-tok-icon-black 2300 Highland Road • Batesville, Arkansas 72501 • Phone: (870) 307-7000 • Fax: (870) 307-7001 2026 © Speak Creative. All rights reserved. Sitemap | Privacy Policy |"
    },
