e and Literature Communication Studies Journalism English Film and Media Studies French Spanish Mabee Simpson Library Math and Computer Science Computer Science Data Science Mathematics Military Science Pre-Health Professions Physics Minor Requirements Physics Courses Pre-Engineering Math & Science Faculty Natural Sciences Biology Chemistry Pre-Dental Pre-Medical Pre-Pharmacy Pre-Physical Therapy Pre-veterinarian Next Steps and Orientation School of Dental Medicine School of Veterinary Medicine Summer Camps Thriving Scots Program TRIO Programs Apple Project Upward Bound Overview and Eligibility How to Apply Academic Session Bridge Component Current Participants and Alumni Employment Opportunities Apple Project Summer Session Upward Bound Math Science Apply for UBMS Academic Year Session Personnel Admissions Admission ACT Prep Camp Admissions Events Honors Fellows Welcome Day Apply for Admission Cost & Aid Tuition and Fees Scholarships & Aid Programs Academic Progress Policy Student Account Policies Financial Aid FAQs Student Debt Forms Helpful Links Meet the Staff Info for First-Year Students Info for Homeschool Students International Student Information Info for Transfer Students Transfer Student Open House Process of Applying as a Transfer Student Next Steps Plan a Visit Submit a Deposit UACCB 2+2 Transfer Agreements Why Lyon? Send Me More Information Campus Life Around Town Army ROTC Campus Clinic Immunization Records and Information Online Resources for Health and Wellness AED Locations Covid-19 Information Counseling Center Information Campus Safety Staff Map of Campus Parking Services Bike Registration Resources Emergency Management Clery Report Q & A Safety Education Center for Career and Professional Development Career Planning Job Search Tools Opportunities Career Pathways Podcast Club Sports Cheer Counseling Center Reasons for Counseling Online Resources Stress Relievers Resources for Faculty and Staff Mental Health Screening Housing & Dining Dining Services Residence Life Lyon Education & Adventure Program Wright Wilderness Experience Outdoor Trips & Events Bouldering Wall Challenge Course Disc Golf Course Hiking and Biking Trail Gallery Rental Gear Waivers & Evals LEAP DEI and EEC Information Lyon League Lyon Lift Mobile App Need Help With An Event? Pet Life Pet Life FAQ Pet Life Press Release Pet Policy Campus Pet Requirements Letter From Presidential Pet Campus Pet Application Form Stay Connected Alumni, Parents, & Friends 1872 Society Alumni Association Alumni Awards Alumni Personnel Donor Honor Roll How to Give Stay Involved Why Your Gift Matters Advancement Honor Roll of Donors Alumni Giving The Honor Roll of Donors Mobile App Student Handbook Student Involvement Campus Employment Religious Life Civic Engagement Lyon College Recyclers Greek Life Alpha Xi Delta Chi Omicron Kappa Sigma Phi Mu Tau Kappa Epsilon SAC Student Government Student Organizations Black Student Association MLK Day MLK Day Program Student Life Dean of Students Fitness Facilities Game Room Cardio Room Free Store SOS Pantry Scots Spirit Wear Store The Social and Honor System Title IX Title IX Personnel Title IX Policy Title IX FAQs File a Title IX Report Title IX Support and Resources The Wheelbarrow Contact Resources Contact Us Event Request Application Give Job Opportunities Marketing Request News Report a Concern Information Technology Information Technology IT by the Numbers IT Announcements FAQs/Knowledgebase Governance Links Footer Right Nav Course & Exam Schedules Stay Connected Style Guide Net Price Calculator The Highlander Newspaper Community Calendar News Request a Transcript Schedule a Tour Mobile menu and search toggle Search We can't wait to meet you Schedule a visit Apply to Lyon What you need to get employers to see you View More How to make effective and efficient resumes and cover letters. View More Tips to start building your network now View More Tips for conducting a great interview View More Job Search Strategies Whether it is a job after graduation or a summer internship, you will need to have the following communication tools completed in order to launch a successful search. Resume Visit our resume and cover letter resource page to get templates and ideas. Schedule an appointment with the Center for Career and Professional Development for a review. LinkedIn Profile Create a LinkedIn profile and begin connecting and building your professional network. LinkedIn also is a great resource for finding job opportunities. Handshake Profile Activate your Lyon College Handshake account Create a profile by completing all of your career interest information. This will help recruiters locate you and it will optimize your job alerts. Upload your resume. Where To Find Jobs and Internships Handshake Handshake is the #1-way college students find jobs. With an activated Handshake account, you will be able to search for thousands of early career job opportunities. Why use Handshake? Jobs &Internships for every student (every major) Personalized feed & custom recommendations for you. Employers eager to connect with you! One-stop shop for all your career building and campus career center needs Interstride Are you interested in jobs or internships out of the US? With an activated Interstride account, you will be able to search for new opportunities and learn what it is like working in other parts of the world. Why use Interstride? Interstride is a portal that offers advice and insights, community, job opportunities, and services to ensure students receive the support they need to succeed. LinkedIn Set up a job alert on LinkedIn to receive timely job leads. The Center for Career and Professional Development will be glad to show you how to set up an alert for targeted job leads. Indeed.com You can also go to Indeed.com to set up job alerts for job opportunities that are right for you. Lyon Opportunities Resource Page Interested in on-campus jobs or internships? Visit our Lyon College Opportunities page. Need Help With Your Job Search? Schedule an appointment with the Center for Career and Professional Development. You can also enroll in our BUS 208: Career Professionalism course . Resume and Cover Letters To conduct an effective job search, you need the following communication tools: Having a resume that highlights your value and talents. A cover letter that will motivate a potential employer to want to talk with you. A LinkedIn profile that establishes your personal brand. A portfolio that highlights all of your great creative work. Create a “brag book”. What is a Personal Brand? A personal brand is a short (one to two sentences) summary of what you do and why you excel at it. Many people develop personal brand statements that they use for introductions and networking. In addition to a brand statement, your personal brand will be defined by your resume, cover letters, LinkedIn and all other social media, and interviewing. Your personal brand will be how you want the world to see you. Resumes The purpose of a resume is to: • Provide your contact information • Showcase your competencies and credentials • Tell a story about your experiences and accomplishments • Generate interest from someone that leads to an interview • Create a positive image for you Resume Do's and Don'ts Do This Don't Do This Focus on skills, knowledge, and accomplishments Misrepresent yourself Keep to a manageable length Include a photograph Write in short phrases Use small font sizes Use strong action verbs Use a lot of graphics Avoid personal pronouns Include any personal data Be consistent, mistake free Use acronyms without info Use a reverse chronology format showing your most recent job first Use buzzwords or abbreviations Resume/CV Templates Traditional Resume: Resume Template Note: Students should ALWAYS request a critique of their resume.  While this template gives a general idea of appropriate resume formatting, it is important to note that no two resumes are alike and that all resumes should be tailored to the position for which you’re applying.  Email us to make an appointment with the Career Center: careercenter@lyon.edu. CV Template While CVs and resumes are similar in many ways, a CV provides a more expansive summary. A CV is common in academic areas where there are lot of publication and research information. While a resume is typically one to two pages, it is acceptable for a CV to be a much longer document. Personal Statement Template The personal statement is typically required when applying to graduate school programs. Your personal statement should provide: Why you want to continue your academic studies. What areas of research you plan to focus on. What makes you unique and someone worthy of graduate program acceptance What career plans you have after graduation. Portfolio/Creative Resume While a traditional resume format is effective and expected when applying for business and technical jobs, a creative resume design can help you when applying for opportunities where you will need to show creative expertise (graphic design, advertising). The Career Services Center recommends student go to Canva or Wix to find creative resume, portfolio, and website templates. Cover Letters: Cover Letter Template The purpose of a cover letter and resume package is to gain the interest of an employer so that they want to interview you. Cover letters should do the following: Explain who you are and what you are currently doing. Highlight how your qualifications match up with the qualifications listed in the job listing. This requires that you take the time to personalize the cover letter to the job. Ask the employer for the opportunity to interview. Tips for college student resumes! Networking Why Should You Network? Learn about new careers and jobs. Learn more about an industry. Learn about living in a certain area Learn about a specific company What to Remember About Networking Before Getting Started Networking is essentially about two things – building relationships and asking for help. Done with authenticity and gratitude, networking can be rewarding and fun. The relationship you build can be lasting. Avoid being transactional. Do not set up a networking conversation under the pretense of asking for a job. Make it easy for someone to help you. Schedule your meeting at a time and place that works for them. What to Remember When You Are In a Networking Meeting Come prepared. Do your research on the person you are meeting, where they work, and the topic that you need help on. Have a list of questions prepared. It is OK to write them down so that you can take notes. Be comfortable answering the “tell me about yourself” question. Schedule a meeting with the Center for Career and Professional Development for practice help. Arrive early. Do not be late. Be yourself. It is understandable to be nervous. Stay calm. You got this. Bring a paper copy of your resume (in case your contact asks for it). Listen intently. Do not become distracted about what is your next question. Listen intently and modify the flow of the conversation based on the information you are receiving. After the Networking Meeting Send a thank you email. Show your appreciation and reference what you learned in the conversation. Take the opportunity to follow up later with your contact. Everyone loves to hear how their advice eventually lead to someone’s success. This follow-up will go a long way to solidifying your relationship. How to Get Started Schedule an appointment with the Center for Career and Professional Development for an ideation session on who would be some great people to connect with. Go to the Lyon College LinkedIn page and check out the Alumni tab. This will lead you to the profiles of all of the Lyon alums. Send a LinkedIn connection request to someone you want to know and get the relationship started. Ask your professors, coaches, friends, and family for advice on who you should talk to. Interviewing There Are Many Types of Interviews Structured interviews with set questions Informal interviews with no set format One-on-one interviews Group/Panel interviews Virtual interviews Informational interviews Serial interviews (meeting many people in one day) Case interviews How to Prepare For an Interview Do your research: Read the job description and understand how you can do the job Research the company so you know more about them Know yourself. Study and retain your education, work experience, and qualifications. Have answers prepared for the most commonly asked questions. Have smart questions for the interviewer. How to Present Yourself Dress professionally to match what is appropriate for the company. Arrive 15 minutes early. Bring paper resumes (old school but helpful). Be attentive, enthusiastic, and calm. For virtual interviews, webcams can matter. Make sure that your webcam is in focus, your background is clean and clear, and your lighting is favorable. If you need to test your webcam before an interview, click here: https://www.veed.io/tools/webcam-test Interviewing Do's and Don'ts Do This Don't Do This Be brief and to the point Ramble too long with answers Correct information errors Argue or ignore a mistake Describe past experiences positively Blame past employers/coworkers Ask for clarification when needed Guess the meaning of a question Turn weaknesses into strengths Dwell on a weakness Ask questions Say that you have no questions Summarize your interests/qualifications Talk too much about you Ask for a decision timetable Not know the next steps Ask for the job Assume they know you want it After the Interview Always send a thank you email within 24 hours to everyone you interviewed with. Thank them for the opportunity and restate your interest in the position. Follow up either after the deadline set by the employer or one week after the interview date. Beware of These Interview Knockout Factors Being unprepared Lack of knowledge needed for the position Inability to express yourself clearly Insufficient evidence of your achievements Visible lack of interest Overly aggressive, conceited, or aloof Asking the wrong question – example: asking what the job pays too early in the interview No confidence and poise Poor personal appearance You Need to Practice Schedule mock interviews with the Center for Career and Professional Development Lyon College Start Your Future Lyon is more than just a college. It's a community distinguished by its academic curriculum, unique honor and social systems, and award-winning professors. Apply Today Contact Resources Contact Us Event Request Application Give Job Opportunities Marketing Request News Report a Concern Footer Right Nav Course & Exam Schedules Stay Connected Style Guide Net Price Calculator The Highlander Newspaper Community Calendar tik-tok-icon-black 2300 Highland Road • Batesville, Arkansas 72501 • Phone: (870) 307-7000 • Fax: (870) 307-7001 2026 © Speak Creative. All rights reserved. Sitemap | Privacy Policy |"
